WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.520
Cellular signaling is a complex system&nbsp;
of communication that governs all basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.400
activities of the cell. There are no cells that&nbsp;
don't do signaling. Bacteria and eukaryotic cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.000
may do signaling slightly differently. But they&nbsp;
still do have an integrated correlated system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.240
that's responsible for triggering functions of&nbsp;
the cell through a series of discrete steps.&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.040
Protein signaling can be dissected into three&nbsp;
basic steps. It starts with the receival of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.640
signal, then transduction of the signal, and&nbsp;
finally, a response. In the first step in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:45.120
process, there's a buildup of a concentration of a&nbsp;
signal or ligand. It occupies the receptors on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:51.520
surface of the cell, and in some cases, inside the&nbsp;
cell. Upon binding of the ligand to the receptor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.120
it may cause the conformational changes&nbsp;
of the receptor and trigger the signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.280
to be transduced from extracellular to&nbsp;
intracellular, followed by a response.&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.600
There are a few characteristics&nbsp;
of all cell signal transduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.920
First, is the specificity of the signaling&nbsp;
molecule. The most important thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.160
the signal is that the signal, which&nbsp;
binds to a receptor in a cell membrane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.880
is specific for a particular receptor, and the&nbsp;
different signal won't bind to the same receptor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.640
This is achieved by high affinity protein-protein&nbsp;
interactions such as hydrogen bonding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.320
electrostatic interactions, non-covalent&nbsp;
interactions and etc, so that a low concentration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.320
of the signal molecule is efficient for binding&nbsp;
to the receptor to trigger the function.&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.680
The next characteristic is amplification.&nbsp;
Very commonly, a cell might have a response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.360
that's just the result of a single&nbsp;
molecule binding a single receptor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.000
However, if the cell wants a large response, for&nbsp;
example, the cell might want to make a lot of ATP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.080
or want to replicate the whole genome,&nbsp;
it will need some kind of amplification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.760
where it can turn up the volume of the signal&nbsp;
rapidly. So frequently in signaling pathways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.920
the cell will go through a cascade of reactions&nbsp;
where the signal might affect an enzyme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.760
Once the cell make that enzyme active, it might&nbsp;
work on many, many copies of another enzyme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.320
And then each of those may work on even more&nbsp;
copies. At some point, a single signaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.920
molecule can result in the cascade of a reaction,&nbsp;
similar to a waterfall coming from just a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.600
molecule of water. The cell will get a large&nbsp;
increase in signal as a result of amplification.&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.400
The next feature or characteristic&nbsp;
of signaling is feedback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.680
Once enough response is achieved, the&nbsp;
cell needs to stop the signaling process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.040
Negative feedback loop provides a mechanism&nbsp;
to slow down some of those steps that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.840
involved in amplification, so that the pathway&nbsp;
is turned on for a prescribed amount of time.&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.560
Finally, the other component of a signaling&nbsp;
network is integration. If we look at a signaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.880
pathway, all these signaling steps are not single.
For example, in the signal transduction that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.440
regulate apoptosis and cell proliferation,&nbsp;
there might be just a single signal that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.960
being received, but other components may have&nbsp;
crosstalk within one pathway. This can set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.840
off the different component in the pathway either&nbsp;
amplifying or turning down a particular signaling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.320
Similar to an electrical circuit, the signal can&nbsp;
trigger not just a single pathway, but a network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.440
of pathway interacts and communicate with each&nbsp;
other, to amplify signals or turn down signals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.720
Hence, integration is an important part of&nbsp;
signaling, because you will often be dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.720
with the integrated function of a number&nbsp;
of pathways to get a particular response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.000
And that actually ends up being one of the&nbsp;
situations where sometimes a particular enzyme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.280
may look like a perfect target for a therapeutic&nbsp;
agent. But if you don't take into account the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.760
integration steps, instead of dealing with&nbsp;
a single pathway, you may be dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.920
crosstalk with a lot of other pathways. What’s&nbsp;
more important is that, in a cell, there is always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.560
compensation from other pathways which might&nbsp;
nullify the therapeutics response that you wanted.&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.280
There are many types of signaling molecules.&nbsp;
They may be small molecules, for example, an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:18.880
amino acid or a phospholipid. Alternatively, they&nbsp;
may be proteins like hormone or cytokine. They may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.160
be carbohydrates. They might take different&nbsp;
forms in terms of their molecular structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.640
But we tend to describe signals by where they come&nbsp;
from. An autocrine signal is a signal that may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.240
come from a cell, but it is signaling to itself.&nbsp;
It may produce a component that is released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.840
through a secretory pathway and stays in the&nbsp;
vicinity of the cell. It is like a self-signaling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.680
Paracrine signaling normally occur where the&nbsp;
signaling molecule is produced by a neighboring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:54.080
cell. Endocrine is usually produced by cells from&nbsp;
a distance, such as from other tissues or organs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.600
and coming through the circulatory system. And&nbsp;
finally, juxtacrine is the only one that is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.360
little odd. The signaling is actually coming&nbsp;
from direct physical contact between 2 cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.480
So, it is not self-signaling within a cell. It&nbsp;
is also not something secreted by another a cell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:21.920
It is a signal that triggered&nbsp;
through physical contact.

